nd the Medicaid-cut vote in a swing district", "Cooke has outraised the incumbent, though he holds a large cash advantage"], "rationale": "Fundamentals point to a narrow Democratic edge in a district that leans Republican, and most expert raters plus prediction markets tilt toward a Cooke flip. All available polls come from Democratic sponsors and show Cooke narrowly ahead, so the forecast stays a modest Democratic lead with wide uncertainty.", "watch": "The data-center controversy and any late independent spending could shift a race this close either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Derrick Van Orden (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Western Wisconsin's swing seat is one of the most competitive House races of the cycle. Republican incumbent Derrick Van Orden is seeking a third term in a district that leans about three points Republican, but the national environment favors Democrats and he has drawn criticism for his closeness to President Trump and his vote on Medicaid cuts. Democrat Rebecca Cooke has outraised him, though Van Orden holds a large cash-on-hand edge and benefits from outside support.\n\nMost expert raters call the race a Tossup or Lean D for a flip, and prediction markets give Cooke roughly a 70 percent chance. The sparse polling, all from Democratic-leaning sponsors, shows Cooke up a few points. The race sits as a narrow Democratic lean, with the data-center debate and late spending the most likely movers.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6512, "margin": 2.53}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6495, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6956, "margin": 3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.726, "margin": 3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.6442, "margin": 3.0}]}